---
/**
 * What the page keeps, what it only reads, and why one session shows one MET
 * figure. Rendered by /science/movement/a-workout; see scienceTopics.ts.
 *
 * The app's workout page is figures only — a number, its unit, one mono line
 * of numbers — and every sentence it used to carry lives here instead. The
 * app links to /science#a-workout from the foot of that page.
 */
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const mets = topicHref("mets-vs-met-h");
const zone2 = topicHref("zone-2");
const volume = topicHref("weekly-volume");
const hrr = topicHref("heart-rate-recovery");
---

<div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-text-tertiary">
    The page, card by card
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {[
      ["What Verve keeps", "MET-hours · minutes · MET · average bpm"],
      ["Effort, in your bands", "light · moderate · vigorous minutes"],
      ["Minute by minute", "the heart-rate trace, read live"],
      ["Recovery · Zone 2", "the fall after the end · minutes in your band"],
      ["This week", "measured moderate and vigorous minutes, against 150"],
    ].map(([k, v]) => (
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-0.5 rounded-xl border border-white/8 bg-white/[0.03] px-3 py-2.5">
        <span class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-text-tertiary">{k}</span>
        <span class="text-[12.5px] text-text-secondary">{v}</span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</div>

<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    Apple and the watch makers show pace, splits and a map, and do it well.
    Verve&rsquo;s workout page shows what only Verve can say about the same
    forty minutes, and it says it in figures. What each figure is, where it
    comes from and what it is not, is here.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What Verve keeps.</strong>
    MET-hours are the session&rsquo;s MET value times its hours: 30 minutes at
    4.4 MET is 2.2 MET-h. The same quantity in minutes is 132 MET-minutes.
    The page prints one of them, never both, because two spellings of one
    number read as two numbers that disagree. The average heart rate is
    Apple Health&rsquo;s over the session. Calories are not shown: what Verve
    could print is an estimate from the MET value and your weight, not a
    measurement, and an estimate on a page of measurements is the one figure
    a reader cannot tell apart. <a href={mets}>METs vs MET-hours</a> has the
    difference between how hard and how much.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Effort, in your bands.</strong>
    Your moderate band runs from your resting heart rate and your age
    (Karvonen), so it is yours, and it moves as they do. A session is scored
    against the band that applied on the day and is never re-scored when the
    band later moves: a finished day is finished. The two dashed lines on the
    minute chart are that band&rsquo;s edges, which is why the three figures
    under the bar do not repeat them. Strength, flexibility and balance always
    read as light here. Heart rate is not a measure of their effort, and
    pretending otherwise would credit a hard set as a slow walk.
    <a href={zone2}>Zone 2</a> has where the band comes from.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Minute by minute.</strong>
    Each bar is a minute&rsquo;s average heart rate, coloured by the band it
    fell in. The trace is read from Apple Health on your phone when the page
    opens, and is not stored by Verve or sent anywhere. A minute without a
    reading is a gap in the chart, not a zero. A workout logged by hand, or
    recorded without a watch, has no trace and the card says so in four words.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Recovery.</strong>
    The fall in heart rate in the first minute after the workout truly ended,
    read from Apple Health&rsquo;s end time rather than the start plus the
    duration. The small line under it is the two readings: the rate at the end
    and the rate a minute later. A walk that ended below your moderate band is
    not read, because there was nothing to recover from, and the card shows a
    dash. The one cited line, and why there is no second one, is on
    <a href={hrr}>heart rate recovery</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Zone 2.</strong>
    The minutes this session spent inside your moderate band, and under it the
    week&rsquo;s running total against the 150 the app asks for. Why 150, and
    why it is a dose rather than a goal, is on <a href={zone2}>Zone 2</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">This week.</strong>
    Moderate and vigorous minutes across the week&rsquo;s aerobic sessions, as
    measured, against the first flag on the Weekly Ascent. This is the same
    figure the Activity tab draws, from the same function, so the two cannot
    disagree. It is not MET-hours: an earlier version of the page summed
    MET-hours over every row in the week, background movement included, and
    read &ldquo;25 of 10&rdquo; under a workout worth 2.2. Verve does not count
    a vigorous minute twice the way the published 150 does, which is why the
    flags on the Ascent carry no organisation&rsquo;s name;
    <a href={volume}>weekly volume</a> has the arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What travels, and what does not.</strong>
    The MET-hours, the band split and the zone 2 minutes are stored on the
    activity row, go into your export, and with sync on reach My health on this
    site, where In depth and the coach&rsquo;s summary read them. The trace and
    the recovery are read live from the phone each time and never leave it.
  </p>
</div>
